      "timestamp_sec": 385,
      "why": "A specific revenue-per-head number plus a strong historical claim."
    },
    {
      "text": "The AI-native companies that I see inside YC encode all of that as skills, written procedures that their agents execute, and they hire they hire engineers whose job it is to maintain those skills, to do the work the skills can't do yet.",
      "timestamp_sec": 385,
      "why": "Defines the AI-native operating model and where humans still sit in it."
    },
    {
      "text": "you actually have to be really, really careful about where the compute computation is actually happening. It's happening almost always in two different places and all of the bugs, all of the AI engineering that we run into that's a problem, it's usually because something is happening in one side of the equation that should be in the other.",
      "timestamp_sec": 461,
      "why": "The talk's most actionable engineering diagnosis."
    },
    {
      "text": "it actually must not live in the context window. The LLM has to do the human part and seat people.",
      "timestamp_sec": 541,
      "why": "Concrete rule for splitting state from judgment, drawn from a real scheduling problem."
    },
    {
      "text": "an AI agent holds a million tokens. That's about a thousand pages.",
      "timestamp_sec": 664,
      "why": "The quantitative hinge for the working-memory argument."
    },
    {
      "text": "Almost every company on the earth is still running an org that's designed for the seven-digit brain.",
      "timestamp_sec": 708,
      "why": "One-line statement of the market opportunity he's pointing founders at."
    },
    {
      "text": "The question that determines whether your agents are geniuses or goldfish is who decides which three books are open on that desk. That's context engineering.",
      "timestamp_sec": 757,
      "why": "Defines context engineering in the talk's own vocabulary."
    },
    {
      "text": "you're right that retrieval is the primitive, the same way Postgres is just B-trees. The hard part is everything around it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 757,
      "why": "Directly answers the 'this is just RAG' objection."
    },
    {
      "text": "Retrieval is easy. Being worth retrieving from is the product.",
      "timestamp_sec": 757,
      "why": "The most quotable formulation of the company-brain thesis."
    },
    {
      "text": "a brain nobody curates becomes a garbage dump with great search. Retrieval will surface a stale fact with total confidence.",
      "timestamp_sec": 838,
      "why": "The steelmanned failure mode, offered by the advocate himself."
    },
    {
      "text": "the primitive is not memory. It's memory plus hygiene, provenance on every fact, contradiction contradiction checks when new information collides with the old, and a librarian, human plus agent, whose actual job is pruning.",
      "timestamp_sec": 879,
      "why": "The prescriptive remedy — concrete requirements for a knowledge layer."
    },
    {
      "text": "Because if you have to ask for something twice, you failed.",
      "timestamp_sec": 930,
      "why": "The single takeaway he tells the audience to keep."
    },
    {
      "text": "The organization that captures what it learns like this gets smarter every single day. The one that doesn't wakes up every morning with amnesia, no matter how good the model is. Model quality is rented, but if you build your brain, you you own that brain.",
      "timestamp_sec": 966,
      "why": "Frames memory as the durable moat versus model choice."
    },
    {
      "text": "Open Claw is the Ferrari, I will always recommend it, but Codex is a really good Honda. It will do 90% of this. Uh it will not blow your face off, but it will get you there. Use whatever. The concepts are the point, not my repos.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1076,
      "why": "Explicitly decouples the argument from his own tooling."
    },
    {
      "text": "A lot of people in the world right now are terrified about what happens to all the jobs, and I understand the fear. But I want to say it plainly, that is a failure of imagination.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1076,
      "why": "Takes a contested side on AI and employment."
    },
    {
      "text": "He built a repo of 80,000 markdown files, a company brain for one small boy, and he pushed himself to the absolute edge of human, what humanity knows about his son's exact condition. No lab, no grant, no permission.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1115,
      "why": "The talk's emotional anchor and its clearest non-commercial application."
    }
